# modules.cpp display architecture

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.0 specifications. This document specifies the
implemented portable display foundation and its physical bindings. mm.display,
MCU SPI, the SSD1680 controller, the pico_epaper and pico_epaper_b board
profiles, and the portable smoke application exist. Both profiles are
code-backed and target buildable; visual refresh and sleep behavior remain
hardware-qualification requirements rather than completed claims.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for the implemented mm.mcu
interface and provider mechanism. docs/modules-platforms.mdy remains
authoritative for SDK and board selection. docs/modules-mm.mdy remains
authoritative for the closed v1.2 manifest schema.

The design uses the existing platform-interface and platform-provider keys,
which already belong to mm: 1.2. It does not add a source-manifest key. A future
key separating a composite profile from its underlying SDK board would require
the next manifest version and is not silently assigned to 1.2 here.

# Decision

Display behavior is a device service layered above MCU transports. It is not a
new group of virtual functions on mm::mcu::Platform.

The intended dependency direction is:

Opening fence
portable application
        |
     mm.display
        |
selected board display provider
        |
ePaper or other controller module
        |
GPIO, SPI, and timer from mm.mcu
        |
selected MCU platform provider
Closing fence

An application using only portable display behavior declares:

Opening fence
use: mm.display
Closing fence

It does not name a controller, vendor, SDK, GPIO number, or provider module.
The selected physical board profile supplies those facts.

# Implemented foundation

mm.display is a platform interface with an allocation-free API for geometry,
initialization, packed one-bit writes, clear, refresh, and sleep. Its fallback
object reports Unsupported, and host tests exercise registration and lifecycle
behavior without selecting a target platform.

mm.epaper.ssd1680 implements the first controller beneath that interface. Its
panel descriptor deliberately leaves initialization bytes, update controls,
dimensions, polarities, and timeouts with the eventual physical provider. This
keeps an unqualified waveform or pinout out of the generic controller.

# Required invariants

- mm.display has one globally unique meaning in the repository.
- mm.display is a core, project-owned platform interface.
- A display provider has a globally unique module name and reaches mm.display
  through its ordinary use closure.
- A provider is injected only when an executable reaches mm.display.
- Standard Pico and Pico W declarations do not claim that an external display
  is attached.
- A board profile binds mm.display only when its hardware definition guarantees
  a particular attached display and wiring.
- Controller protocol code depends on portable MCU facilities, not directly on
  Pico SDK headers.
- Project and external CMake link paths receive the same provider closure.
- An application that does not use mm.display receives no display provider or
  controller objects.
- A missing provider makes a display-using application unavailable before
  compilation or linking.

# Portable interface

The public interface is intentionally smaller than a graphics toolkit. It owns
display lifecycle, geometry, packed pixel transfer, refresh, and sleep. Text,
fonts, shapes, image decoding, and UI policy belong in modules layered above it.

The public shape is:

Opening fence
enum class Color { White, Black, Red };
enum class Refresh { Full, Partial };

struct Geometry {
    unsigned int width;
    unsigned int height;
    unsigned int bits_per_pixel;
};

struct Rectangle {
    unsigned int x;
    unsigned int y;
    unsigned int width;
    unsigned int height;
};

class Display {
public:
    virtual Geometry geometry() const;
    virtual Status initialize();
    virtual Status clear(Color);
    virtual Status write(Rectangle, span<const byte>);
    virtual Status refresh(Refresh);
    virtual Status sleep();
};

void set_display(Display&);
Display& selected_display();
Closing fence

The base implementations return empty geometry or Unsupported. A retained
provider registers its object with set_display; selected_display always returns
either that object or the linkable fallback.

For a one-bit display, pixels are row-major and each row begins at a byte
boundary. The most significant bit is the leftmost pixel, one means white, and
zero means black. The low unused bits of a final partial byte are outside the
visible rectangle and have no portable meaning. Rectangle coordinates use the
visible top-left origin. write modifies controller memory but does not refresh the
panel; refresh is always explicit. A provider for another bits-per-pixel value
must document its equally complete packing before exposing that geometry. The
API does not make a per-pixel bus transaction the portable primitive.

A full framebuffer is optional. Small systems may stream packed rows or tiles;
applications with enough memory may build a framebuffer in a higher layer. The
display interface must not require every target to reserve the largest panel's
framebuffer.

Status belongs to the display domain and preserves distinctions useful to a
caller, including unsupported refresh mode, invalid region, not initialized,
busy timeout, and transport failure. A provider maps mm.mcu failures into that
domain without discarding the cause needed for diagnostics.

# Provider binding

mm.display carries the existing marker:

Opening fence
mm: 1.2
kind: module
name: display
module: mm.display
platform-interface:
file: display.cppm
Closing fence

A concrete hardware profile binds it to one unique provider:

Opening fence
platform-provider: mm.display platform.pico_epaper.display
Closing fence

The provider imports mm.display, the selected controller module, and mm.mcu. It
owns the fixed panel descriptor and wiring, constructs the controller, and
registers the Display implementation from a retained static object. Existing
provider validation, board-over-SDK precedence, conditional closure injection,
and core platform-edge rules apply unchanged.

An SDK must not provide a default display binding merely because it can drive
one. The SDK supplies buses; attachment is a board-profile fact. A board binding
may override an SDK binding under the existing rule, but the normal design has
no SDK display binding to override.

# Board and device discovery

The implemented mm::mcu::Board describes GPIOs and an onboard LED. Future board
metadata may add typed, read-only inventories of attached devices, but metadata
does not replace the functional mm.display interface.

An optional display inventory may expose facts such as stable name, geometry,
color capability, and controller identity. It must not expose vendor SDK types
or pointers whose lifetime is shorter than the provider. Heterogeneous devices
must not be represented by a growing union of behavior in mm::mcu::Platform.

Standard development boards remain descriptions of the board itself. An ePaper
module connected by jumper wires is application configuration unless the user
selects a composite profile that fixes the wiring. An application may directly
construct a controller with an explicit wiring descriptor for experimentation,
but that application is intentionally hardware-specific and is not evidence of
portable board binding.

# Composite board profiles

A fixed carrier, HAT, or documented board-plus-panel assembly is represented by
a distinct board profile. The implemented profiles are:

Opening fence
pico_epaper    Pico-ePaper-2.66, black and white
pico_epaper_b  Pico-ePaper-2.66-B, black, white, and red
Closing fence

Both profiles derive from the Pico SDK board pico. They therefore inherit
pico-arm, the RP2040 processor identity, and platform.pico.mcu, while each adds
its own mm.display binding. The generated board chain lets the Pico external
bridge map either profile to PICO_BOARD=pico without teaching the bridge either
composite name.

The boards document the two Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.66 variants attached to the
headers, using SPI1 at 4 MHz with CLK GP10, DIN GP11, CS GP9, DC GP8, reset GP12,
and busy GP13. Each provider exposes the panel in its native 152 by 296
orientation and does not enable partial refresh until its LUT and recovery
sequence are qualified.

Panel selection is configuration, not runtime discovery. The carrier exposes
DIN but no SPI receive or panel-identification signal, and BUSY reports only
controller activity. configure --board pico_epaper selects monochrome;
configure --board pico_epaper_b selects the B panel. A timing guess is not a
valid substitute for that authored hardware fact.

# Core boundary

A core application declares only the core interface. A non-core Pico provider
may enter its link as selected platform implementation under the exact
platform-edge rule already specified for mm.mcu. That does not permit the
application or another core module to declare a third-party library dependency.

Project-authored controller modules may remain core when they use only project
interfaces. A wrapper over a vendored controller library follows the library
rules, carries a lib. module name, and is non-core. A board provider using that
wrapper remains platform-supplied implementation; the dependency must still be
visible in its authored use closure.

# Model and documentation

The abstract model exposes mm.display as a marked interface, each authored
platform-provider declaration, and the effective binding for a configured
platform. The application's authored uses remain unchanged. Provider and
controller closure augmentation is derived platform state rather than an
invented source declaration.

Board documentation names the physical display, controller, geometry, colors,
and wiring source. Controller documentation owns command-set and refresh
limitations. Generated documentation must clearly distinguish an unimplemented
profile from a hardware-qualified implementation.

# Implementation stages

1. Implemented: add and test the MCU SPI facility described by
   docs/modules-epaper.mdy.
2. Implemented: add mm.display with a host stand-in and lifecycle tests.
3. Implemented: add mm.epaper.ssd1680 against fake GPIO, SPI, and timer
   services.
4. Implemented: add pico_epaper and pico_epaper_b with distinct providers for
   the monochrome and black/white/red Waveshare Pico-ePaper-2.66 variants.
5. Implemented for the Pico external link: add apps/epaper-smoke using only
   mm.display and build it through the selected provider closure.
6. Pending hardware: qualify initialization, full refresh, supported partial refresh, timeout,
   sleep, rebuild, UF2 validation, and physical flashing on hardware.

# Acceptance criteria

- An app importing only mm.display links with exactly one selected display
  provider and the provider's controller closure.
- The same app is unavailable on a board with no display binding.
- An app not importing mm.display contains no display provider or controller
  registration object.
- A standard pico or pico-w selection never implies an attached panel.
- A composite profile reports its underlying MCU board and attached display
  separately.
- Model output distinguishes authored imports, selected platform binding, and
  injected provider closure.
- Host unit tests require no Pico SDK, cross compiler, picotool, panel, or USB
  device.
- Hardware qualification names the exact board, panel revision, controller,
  wiring, toolchain, and observed refresh modes.

# What this does not settle

- SSD1680 is the first implemented controller and the two Pico-ePaper-2.66
  variants are the first code-backed physical profiles. Their full-refresh and
  sleep behavior are not reported as hardware-qualified until observed on each
  named assembly.
- Multi-display selection and hot-plug discovery are not specified.
- A general hardware-profile manifest schema is not added to v1.2.
- Fonts, drawing primitives, image formats, UI layout, and animation are outside
  mm.display.
- Color values beyond the panel's discrete pigments are not promised.
- Partial refresh is a capability, not a requirement of every controller.
